From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 2026 18:21:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07241/11362] [outer-loop] bp35 and lf52 fully solved 9/9 and
 10/10 (gpt-5.6-sol, max effort)

Two independent codex sessions, run concurrently, each achieved a full game
clear on an ARC-AGI-3 game this project had been working for 25+ rounds
(bp35) and 14+ rounds (lf52) without reaching completion.

BP35 (round 26, live_agent_self_discovery, frames-only, no source read):
built directly on round 25's discovery that flight survival is not governed
by a "viewport-crossing" rule. Isolated the real discriminator via a matched
A/B control -- the SAME switch click from the SAME start lands safely in 22
layers over a closed door but dies in 47 layers over a pre-opened one. The
missing move was REMOTE-opening a door before entering its column, then
building/riding a body through it -- this single insight unlocked the entire
previously-impossible [-48,-13] dead zone, a color-12 "ceiling" selector
leading to an upper tower, a horizontal door crossing the old cols6-12
divider, and a final 9-switch bank. Won on action 410 (342-action banked
prefix + a new 68-action tail). bp35: 8/9 -> 9/9.

LF52 (L8-L10, development_proxy -- honestly disclosed source-reading after a
genuine structural wall, matching the exact precedent that broke this same
game's L7 11-round deadlock in round 13): L8's adapter-free search exhausted
a 35-state jump graph and 1,511 carrier-inclusive states with no win before
switching to reading the permitted public source
(environment_files/lf52/271a04aa/lf52.py) to find 2 off-screen relay pegs.
L9 and L10 introduced further new mechanics (camera-reveal-on-carrier-load,
off-screen leapfrog relays, 8-carrier simultaneous routing). Won on action
1009 (759-action banked L7 prefix + 68 + 100 + 82-action level suffixes).
lf52: 7/10 -> 10/10.

Given the magnitude of these claims (two full game clears, the largest
possible outcome for this investigation), did NOT trust the session reports
alone. For EACH win, independently before committing:
- Replayed the exact claimed action sequence 5x from completely fresh
  environments using separately-written verification code (not the codex
  session's own scripts) -- both reached GameState.WIN deterministically,
  5/5.
- Independently recomputed every claimed sha256 checksum from the raw action
  data -- all matched exactly.
- Called the official arc_solver_kit.reproduce() gate myself for both games
  -- both returned reproduced=true at the claimed level.
- Ran scripts/adversarial_verify.py against both artifacts. bp35 raised one
  CRITICAL flag (ARC_OUTER_LOOP_SOLVE "already registered") -- diagnosed as
  a same-session ordering false positive (the artifact's own registry update
  ran before the flag check re-read the file) by reading the linter's source
  and confirming via `git diff` that the registry's 8->9 bump was THIS
  round's own uncommitted change, not a pre-existing credit; lf52 passed
  clean outright (its development_proxy provenance is correctly exempted
  from that same check). lf52's win_levels total (10) was also independently
  confirmed against the registry's own established value before trusting
  "full game clear".
- Spawned two independent hostile-reviewer sub-agents (one per game) with
  explicit instructions to hunt for over-claims, provenance mislabeling,
  plan/execution conflation, and internal arithmetic inconsistency. Both
  returned CLEAN verdicts; the bp35 reviewer independently re-diagnosed the
  linter false-positive from source rather than taking my explanation on
  faith, and confirmed via registry history that this is genuinely the
  first-ever L9 win after 25 straight honest-null rounds, not a duplicate.
- Read every narrative/novel_mechanics_found/bounded_negative_results section
  for internal consistency and appropriate epistemic hedging.

Both sessions ran concurrently against the same shared ops/ files
(registry.yaml, changelog.md, metrics.md); reviewed the interleaved diffs
before staging and confirmed they are cleanly additive and mutually
consistent (aggregate reproducible_total_levels chains correctly across
both sessions' edits: 179->180->181->182->183).

Evidence:
- results/outer_loop_round26_bp35_probe_l9_20260717.json
- results/outer_loop_lf52_l8plus_probe_20260717.json

